@* HelpTwoFactorPage: help topic "Two-factor sign-in" at "/help/two-factor". Static text and a screenshot of the
   two-factor card of the Account page. *@
@page "/help/two-factor"

<HelpTopicFrame Slug="two-factor">
    <section class="card">
        <p><strong>Two-factor sign-in</strong> adds a code from an authenticator app on your phone to your password. A
            stolen or guessed password alone then no longer opens your account. It is optional, and it is for accounts
            with a ReviewCenter password; an account that signs in with an identity provider is protected by the
            provider instead.</p>
        <h2>Turn it on</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Open the <strong>Account</strong> page and find the <strong>Two-factor authentication</strong> card.</li>
            <li>Type your password and press <strong>Set up two-factor sign-in</strong>.</li>
            <li>In an authenticator app (any app that makes time-based codes will do), add an account with the key that
                is shown. Apps that accept an address can use the <code>otpauth://</code> address below the key. The key
                is shown only now, while you set up; it is not shown again.</li>
            <li>Type the 6-digit code the app shows and press <strong>Turn on</strong>.</li>
            <li>ReviewCenter then shows <strong>10 recovery codes</strong>, <strong>once</strong>. Save them somewhere
                safe, apart from your password (a password manager or a printout). Press
                <strong>I have saved them</strong> when you have.</li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="help-figure">
            <img class="help-shot" src="/help/two-factor-1.png" loading="lazy"
                 alt="The Two-factor authentication card of the Account page with two-factor sign-in on, the number of recovery codes left, and the Get new recovery codes and Turn off sections." />
            <figcaption>The Two-factor authentication card of the Account page, with two-factor sign-in on.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h2>Sign in</h2>
        <p>Sign in with your user name and password as always. A second page then asks for a code: type the current
            code from the app and press <strong>Sign in</strong>. A code works once. The second step must be finished
            within 5 minutes; if it takes longer, or the answer is wrong, you are told only that the code is not valid or
            the sign-in step has expired, and you start again.</p>
        <h2>If you lose your phone</h2>
        <p>On the second page you can type one of your <strong>recovery codes</strong> instead of the 6-digit code. Each
            recovery code works once. After you are in, turn two-factor sign-in off and set it up again with the new
            phone, or press <strong>Get new recovery codes</strong> when few are left. The card shows how many you have
            left. If you have neither the phone nor a recovery code, ask a system administrator to reset your two-factor
            sign-in (see below).</p>
        <h2>Signing a review</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>To sign, you type your password <strong>and</strong> a current code from the app. The code is only for the
                signature; recovery codes cannot sign.</li>
            <li>A code works only once, also across sign-in and signing. If you used the code of the current
                30-second period to sign in, <strong>wait for the next code</strong> before you sign.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Turn it off, or get new recovery codes</h2>
        <p>Open the <strong>Turn off two-factor sign-in</strong> section of the card, type your password and a current code
            (or a recovery code), and press <strong>Turn off</strong>. <strong>Get new recovery codes</strong> needs your
            password and a current code; the old recovery codes stop working. The new ones are shown once.</p>
        <h2>An administrator resets it</h2>
        <p>A system administrator can press <strong>Reset two-factor</strong> next to a user on the <strong>Users</strong>
            page. That removes the second factor and the recovery codes of that user, who can then sign in with the
            password alone and set it up again. Project administrators cannot do this.</p>
        <h2>Good to know</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Wrong codes count like wrong passwords: by default, after 5 failed attempts the account is locked for 5 minutes.</li>
            <li>Turning two-factor sign-in off, or an administrator resetting it, does not sign anybody out; sessions that
                are already open stay valid.</li>
            <li>API tokens are not affected. They never need a code, and they still cannot sign.</li>
            <li>The server's settings can switch the whole feature off (<code>Identity:Totp:Enabled</code> set to
                <code>false</code>). Then nobody is asked for a code, so <strong>accounts that had it turned on are
                protected by the password alone</strong> until it is switched on again. Tell the person who runs the
                server if you rely on it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">Setting up, turning off, resetting, new recovery codes, recovery code use, and refused codes are
            recorded in the audit trail, never the codes themselves.</p>
    </section>
</HelpTopicFrame>
